3\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672807 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672807 1083 #uuid "1d9ed5b4-4935-583e-a40d-801dceb367ef" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672808 1080 "228.5\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672808 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672808 1083 #uuid "21e1e846-9e05-501b-974e-8642dacc9f03" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672809 1080 "381.6\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672809 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672809 1083 #uuid "25f1ca40-bbb2-585e-b802-e7abc4b0609a" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672810 1080 "238.8\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672810 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672810 1083 #uuid "37d3dbfe-81e6-5c4b-bc37-f60fb4beb15e" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672811 1080 "241.4\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672811 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672811 1083 #uuid "3845663d-6c8b-564f-991e-5525391b388d" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672812 1080 "379.9\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672812 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672812 1083 #uuid "3f66ff27-185c-5ed0-968e-616134064bcd" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672813 1075 #edb/ref 17592186672815 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672813 1075 #edb/ref 17592186672818 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672813 1075 #edb/ref 17592186672820 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672813 1075 #edb/ref 17592186672822 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672813 1075 #edb/ref 17592186672832 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672813 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672813 1077 "selection" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672813 1078 #edb/ref 17592186672822 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672813 1079 #uuid "47f0a520-4075-560e-ac9e-5f9c2b76fd9e" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672814 1068 #edb/ref 17592186672835 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672814 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672814 1071 "A saline reservoir for a medical research lab can hold up to $190\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution. Initially, it contains $50\\,\\text{gal}$ of saline solution with $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved salt. A more concentrated saline solution with concentration $1.2\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $10\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672814 1072 #uuid "4d24b72e-d25f-59d2-bb3e-c6bd67f97b9c" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672814 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the reservoir minus the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.2\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt enters the reservoir at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.2\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.2\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =18\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $50\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $15\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $10\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=50+15t-10t=5(10+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $10\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(10\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{10+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =18-\\frac{2Q}{10+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{10+t}\\,Q=18.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(10+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|10+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(10+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(10+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{10+t}\\,Q) & =(10+t)^{2}\\cdot 18 \\\\ (10+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(10+t)Q & =18(10+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(10+t)^{2}) & =18(10+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(10+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 18(10+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(10+t)^{2} & =18\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(10+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =6(10+t)+\\frac{C}{(10+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=20,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =20 \\\\ 6(10+0)+\\frac{C}{(10+0)^{2}} & =20 \\\\ 60+\\frac{C}{(10)^{2}} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(10)^{2}} & =-40 \\\\ C & =-40(10)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =6(10+t)-\\frac{40(10)^{2}}{(10+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =6(10+t)-40{\\left(\\frac{10}{10+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $190\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=190$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =190 \\\\ 50+15{t}_{o}-10{t}_{o} & =190 \\\\ 50+5{t}_{o} & =190 \\\\ 5{t}_{o} & =140 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =28\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(28) & =6(10+28)-40{\\left(\\frac{10}{10+28}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =228-40{\\left(\\frac{10}{38}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈225.2\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672815 1080 "73.2\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672815 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672815 1083 #uuid "50c05219-e146-5c0b-9f47-8478e9cc17eb" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672816 1075 #edb/ref 17592186672810 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672816 1075 #edb/ref 17592186672811 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672816 1075 #edb/ref 17592186672823 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672816 1075 #edb/ref 17592186672825 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672816 1075 #edb/ref 17592186672827 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672816 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672816 1077 "selection" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672816 1078 #edb/ref 17592186672827 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672816 1079 #uuid "53445c49-a61b-5717-8a8f-2ba5cfb737e8" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672817 1068 #edb/ref 17592186672816 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672817 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672817 1071 "An ink reservoir for an industrial printer can hold up to $249\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of ink solution with $80\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved ink dye. A more concentrated ink solution with concentration $1.0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672817 1072 #uuid "566ddc14-35df-5f2a-8371-ba5cac1be4c4" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672817 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which ink dye enters the reservoir minus the rate at which ink dye leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which ink dye enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of ink dye enters the reservoir at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *ink dye* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =9\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which ink dye exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which ink dye leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $9\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=120+9t-6t=3(40+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of ink dye in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{3(40+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *ink dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(40+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(40+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6Q}{3(40+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{40+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =9-\\frac{2Q}{40+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{40+t}\\,Q=9.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(40+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|40+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(40+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(40+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(40+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(40+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{40+t}\\,Q) & =(40+t)^{2}\\cdot 9 \\\\ (40+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(40+t)Q & =9(40+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(40+t)^{2}) & =9(40+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(40+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 9(40+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(40+t)^{2} & =9\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(40+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =3(40+t)+\\frac{C}{(40+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $80\\,\\text{lb}$ of ink dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=80,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =80 \\\\ 3(40+0)+\\frac{C}{(40+0)^{2}} & =80 \\\\ 120+\\frac{C}{(40)^{2}} & =80 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{40}^{2}} & =-40 \\\\ C & =-40(40)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =3(40+t)-\\frac{40(40)^{2}}{(40+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =3(40+t)-40{\\left(\\frac{40}{40+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $249\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=249$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =249 \\\\ 120+9{t}_{o}-6{t}_{o} & =249 \\\\ 120+3{t}_{o} & =249 \\\\ 3{t}_{o} & =129 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =43\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(43) & =3(40+43)-40{\\left(\\frac{40}{40+43}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =249-40{\\left(\\frac{40}{83}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈239.7\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672818 1080 "77.0\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672818 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672818 1083 #uuid "58e5327d-e065-5983-b7c0-80260b2d7837" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672819 1080 "378.8\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672819 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672819 1083 #uuid "700b78b2-e9d9-511c-b04d-6cf0070d3cd6" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672820 1080 "75.1\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672820 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672820 1083 #uuid "79055611-d13a-5b4e-b215-47c495ddcd57" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672821 1080 "495.0\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672821 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672821 1083 #uuid "80951659-772c-51af-88a0-1b9a88f45896" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672822 1080 "72.9\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672822 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538708 17592186045443 true] [17592186672822 1083 #uuid "815ad045-92f6-